# 35. Conclusion

The first six pillars have demonstrated that the United States already possesses substantial conservation assets.

It has:

* public lands;
* conservation laws;
* scientific institutions;
* monitoring networks;
* restoration programs;
* fisheries-management systems;
* wildlife programs;
* water institutions;
* state and Tribal governments;
* private conservation capacity.

The remaining question is whether these assets can be converted into durable ecological outcomes.

Institutional capacity is the bridge.

The relevant question is not simply:

> "How many people work in conservation?"

It is:

> **"Does the conservation system have the right people, expertise, administrative systems, organizational structures, and institutional memory to carry out its responsibilities effectively?"**

The preliminary answer is that capacity should be treated as a **performance variable**, not merely an administrative consideration.

A conservation institution with insufficient capacity may be unable to use:

* available science;
* statutory authority;
* appropriated funding;
* partnerships;
* technology.

Conversely, a well-designed institution can sometimes achieve more by improving coordination, expertise, and implementation than by simply expanding programs.

The emerging principle is:

> **Conservation capacity is the ability of institutions to convert authority, knowledge, resources, and partnerships into sustained action and measurable ecological outcomes.**

That principle will guide the next phase of research.
